  "argument": "arg-lq-wien-integration",
  "title": "Wien's law solved for the temperature",
  "spoken": "One over the temperature equals minus one over B times the frequency, times the natural logarithm of the density over A times the frequency cubed.",
  "explanation": "Taking the logarithm of Wien's law and solving for one over the temperature expresses the temperature through the density. With one over T equal to the entropy's slope, this is what gets integrated.",
  "review": "draft",
  "notation": "modern-pedagogical",
  "unitSystem": "si",
  "assumptions": [
    "Dilute radiation: the density is low enough, relative to temperature, that Wien's law describes the spectrum.",
    "These are modern teaching equations in SI notation, with A and B for the paper's alpha and beta; they are not a transcription of the printed paper."
